# DirectObservableAwaitSites.allow — the ratchet for `await <an observable>`.
#
# Each line is a file that still awaits an observable DIRECTLY:
#
#     var node = await stream.Take(1).Timeout(TestTimeouts.Convergence);
#
# WHY THE SHAPE IS A DEFECT, and why it is the SAME defect as `.ToTask()` rather than the cure for
# it. `await` on an IObservable<T> binds Rx's own GetAwaiter() extension, which builds an
# AsyncSubject<T> and completes the continuation from inside OnCompleted — INLINE, on whichever
# thread signalled, still inside Rx's trampoline. Everything the continuation then does inherits
# that scheduler, and because `await` captures TaskScheduler.Current when there is no
# SynchronizationContext, so does every LATER await in the same method. That is #2301 (a grain
# teardown holding the scheduler its own deactivation needed) and #2377 (a query walk enqueued on a
# trampoline that could only drain after the block waiting for it returned).
# InlineResumptionMechanismTest MEASURES all of this rather than asserting it: the bridge and the
# direct await resume on the producer; ObserveCompletion and Await do not.
#
# WHY THERE ARE 228 OF THEM — this inventory was ASKED FOR. Until 2026-09-18, AGENTS.md, the
# /async skill, the /testing skill and Doc/Architecture/AsynchronousCalls all prescribed "await the
# observable directly with a `.Timeout(...)`" as the way OFF `.ToTask()`, while
# ReactiveTestAssertions, WritingTests, ScriptExecution and RemovingObservableToTaskBridges said the
# exact opposite and correctly. The change that adds this file corrects the four. The sites they
# produced may only shrink.
#
# WHAT THE GUARD SEES is a STATEMENT, not a line. The awaited expression runs from the keyword to a
# `;`, a `,` or a closing bracket AT ITS OWN DEPTH, so a chain spread over four lines is one
# expression; a `<…>` after an identifier is absorbed whole so its commas do not truncate it. The
# rule then reads the expression's TAIL against ObservableTails. That list is LOAD-BEARING in the
# same way HubReachableAsyncGuard.MeshOperations is: an Rx operator missing from it is a tail the
# scan cannot name, and those sites are silently uncounted.
#
# THE FIX AT A SITE, in order of preference:
#   1. Stay reactive — Subscribe(onNext, onError), or assert through MeshWeaver.Reactive.Assertions
#      (`await x.Should().Within(TestTimeouts.Convergence).Emit("because")`), which bridges once,
#      correctly, inside the assertion.
#   2. `.Await(ct)` — MeshWeaver.Messaging.ObservableAwait. A faithful ToTask (LAST value, faults on
#      an empty sequence); where the chain already reduces to one element, LAST is FIRST and nothing
#      but the scheduling changes.
#   3. `.ObserveCompletion(reportLateFault, ct)` — the same guarantee plus an error arm that stays
#      attached, for a fault that lands after the wait settled. Never pass an empty lambda.
#
# THIS RATCHET MAY ONLY SHRINK. A new file, a raised count, or a raised total is a failure. Lower
# ObservableToTaskBridgeGuard.DirectAwaitTotalBudget whenever you delete or lower an entry. A line
# that has gone stale is REPORTED, not failed — two PRs shrinking concurrently would otherwise red
# main on whichever merged second.
#
# Scope: test/, and nothing else. src/, tools/, samples/, clients/ AND memex/ are held at ZERO by
# NoProductionCodeAwaitsAnObservableDirectly, with no allow file and no line to add — the first four
# reached zero on 2026-09-18 when MeshWeaver.Testing.InMesh.MeshTestContext.First, the last one, was
# fixed, and memex/ JOINED that set when its five DevLogin sign-in sites were converted (#4756).
# test/MeshWeaver.Messaging.Hub.Test/InlineResumptionMechanismTest.cs is NOT listed: it is exempt,
# because it is the file whose entire purpose is to exhibit the shape and measure it, and the
# exemption is verified rather than trusted by TheExemptedPinningTestStillPinsTheShape.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# memex/ — GONE FROM THIS FILE, because the ROOT moved rather than the line being deleted (#4756).
# It held 5 sites in one file, all in the DevLogin sign-in action: a genuine ASP.NET edge where a
# bridge belongs, counted because the bridge it used was the WRONG one. All five now carry
# `.Await(HttpContext.RequestAborted)`, so the sign-in action's remainder no longer runs on whichever
# mesh thread answered. `memex/` is PRODUCTION code, and a ratchet is the wrong home for production
# code: a ratchet promises "this may only shrink", and what is wanted is "there are none" — so the
# root is now in DirectAwaitZeroRoots and the next one added there is refused with no line to add.
#
# 🚨 AwaitedMeshReadSites.allow still budgets DevAuthController.cs at 1, and that is NOT stale. It is
# a different question — awaiting a mesh READ at all, whatever the bridge — and its own note says the
# migration means "reshaping the sign-in action around a subscription", which this change did not do.
# The two counts answering differently is the instrument gap the previous note described, not a
# mistake in either file.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# test/ — 223 sites in 66 files. Every one is a suite waiting on the mesh, and the thread it
# resumes on is the thread that then runs the rest of the test, its mesh teardown, and — under
# xUnit — the runner starting the next class. That is why a bridge "only in a test" changes what
# the test measures (#2301, #2377), and why the retracted test exemption was never safe.
